name: project-governance
description: Project housekeeping rules for {{ project_name }} - keep CLAUDE.md, README, CHANGELOG, version, and commit hygiene in sync on every change.
---

Apply these rules on every change to this project, without being asked:

## Documentation

- **CLAUDE.md**: after any change to architecture, conventions, dependencies, or how the
  project is run, update CLAUDE.md so it stays an accurate map of the project for the next
  session. Do not let it drift.
- **README.md**: keep it accurate for a human reader - installation, usage, and any
  user-facing behavior that changed.

## Versioning

- The project version lives in {% if stack == 'node' %}`package.json` under `"version"`{% else %}`pyproject.toml` under `[project].version`{% endif %}. Follow semantic
  versioning: patch for fixes, minor for backward-compatible features, major for breaking
  changes.
- On every notable change, bump the version and add an entry to `CHANGELOG.md` following the
  Keep a Changelog format (`### Added` / `### Changed` / `### Fixed` / `### Removed` under a
  new version heading). Do this in the same commit as the change it describes.

## Commits

- Commit each new feature or meaningful change on its own, with a Conventional Commit
  message (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`, ...). Do not batch unrelated
  changes into one commit.
- **Never run `git push`.** It is blocked by this project's `.claude/settings.json` and
  should stay that way. Commits stay local until a human decides to push them.

## Tests

- `.githooks/pre-commit` runs the linter and the full test suite before a commit is
  accepted; a failing commit means the change is not ready, not something to bypass with
  `--no-verify`.
- Keep the test suite itself up to date: every new feature or bug fix needs a test that
  would fail without it.
